Gunderson and Semeraro~\cite[Question~25]{GunSem} who asked 
\begin{question}
\label{qsn:gs}
    For which $n\equiv0\pmod{4}$ does there exist a $3$-$(n,4,n/4)$ design with the property that every set of $5$ vertices contains either $0$ or $2$ hyperedges?
\end{question}
As we show in Example~\ref{ex:skew3}, it follows from Theorem~\ref{thm:des} that such a $3$-$(n,4,n/4)$ design exists whenever there exists a skew-symmetric conference matrix of order $4n$.
It is conjectured in \cite{S78} that skew-symmetric conference matrices of order $4n$ exist for all $n \in \mathbb N$.
The validity of this conjecture would thus completely answer the question of Gunderson and Semeraro, and would furthermore provide a partial answer to \cite[Problem~1]{FF}.
